title: c1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** One pattern: split the walking companion's job in three. Anchoring attention goes to the System. Company stays with a person. Safety stays with staff, whom the System calls. Staff then need not walk every walk, and no walk is taken with the voice alone.

= Team Design Pattern - Voice in the pocket, person in reach [TDP-01] =

**Report Format:** §2.4 Team Design Patterns (Task Level)

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TDP-01
|Name|Voice in the pocket, person in reach
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature and group reasoning only. Not yet evaluated.

* Derived from SA-01, SA-02, SA-04, PS-01, PS-02, UC01
* Anchoring by cues: HFC-02, HFC-03
* Company as the reason to go out: SA-04, Victor (2012)
* Human handover when automated support runs out: the earlier concept's TDP-02, and Braley et al. (2019) on people with dementia needing human contact when automated prompting confuses
)))
|Problem|A resident with dementia needs an anchor to walk: someone who keeps place and purpose. Staff cannot walk every walk. Partners and volunteers can walk, but cannot anchor a confused resident or handle an emergency alone. Who carries what?
|Solution Structure|(((
1. Resident walks, chooses, stops.
1. Companion walks along and talks.
1. System anchors attention, invites memories, guides back, calls help.
1. Care worker stays in reach: alerts, physical help, distress, exits.
)))
|Solution Description|The companion's job, split. Anchoring - noticing, re-orienting, the way back - goes to the System (F2). Company stays with a person: partner, volunteer, relative. Safety stays with staff, called by the System (F4). Activity coordinator prepares the ground: profile, pairs, script.
|Per-Actor allocation|(((
**Resident (ST-01):**

1. Decide to go; set the pace; stop, sit, turn back.
1. Pause the voice when they like.

**Companion (ST-04, ST-07 or ST-03):**

1. Walk along; talk; take up memory invitations.
1. Pause the voice when the talk is good.
1. Pull the cord when worried. Not responsible for the resident's safety.

**Care worker (ST-02):**

1. Clip on the guide; check it reports ready.
1. Answer alerts: judge, go, help.
1. Walk with residents beyond the garden.

**Activity coordinator (ST-08):**

1. Write profiles with families: welcome and avoided topics.
1. Pair walkers, with both residents' consent.
1. Review cues that do not land.

**System:**

1. F1: invite at the door.
1. F2: present-moment cues, memory invitations, bench practice, orienting cue, way back, welcome back.
1. F4: alert on gate, cord, impact, no response.
1. F5: four-item record.
)))
|Consequences: benefits|Walks with lighter staffing; walks with partners and volunteers become possible; an anchor that needs no memory; staff called when needed. Expected, not observed: UC01-F1-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F4-CL7, UC01-F2-CL8.
|Consequences: risks|(((
* Companions lean on the guide and stop watching
* Walks drift towards voice-only (VT-07); M-10 guards against it
* Gate alerts arrive late, or too often (Open item O-3)
* Memory invitations overheard, or painful (VT-03, UC01-F2-CL5)
)))
|Related Patterns|IDP-01 Present-moment cue; IDP-02 Memory invitation; IDP-03 Two-option offer. TDP-02 (previous concept) shares the human handover.
|Connection to Claims|Rationale for UC01-F1-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F4-CL7, UC01-F2-CL8. Tested against UC01-F2-CL5.
|Validation path|EM-01 (does the hand-off work), EM-02 (does walking rise), EM-03 (do staff and companions accept the split). Second instance: UC02 at dusk, with the care worker starting and the System carrying the practice.

== Open items to check or research ==

|=Item|=What is needed|=Why it matters
|Garden policy (O-7)|Agree with ST-05 and ST-06 who may walk with a partner instead of staff.|Without it, the allocation has no permission.
|Alert thresholds (O-3)|Pilot EM-01; set gate and no-response timing with care workers.|Too late is unsafe; too often is ignored.
|Companion briefing|Short instruction for partners, volunteers and families: pause button, cord, what the guide does not do.|Companions must not assume the guide watches over the resident.

== Graph edges ==

allocates → SA-01 tasks to ST-01 and companions, F1, F2, F4, F5 to System, safety to ST-02; supports → UC01-F1-CL1, UC01-F2-CL2, UC01-F4-CL7, UC01-F2-CL8; tested-against → UC01-F2-CL5.

== References ==

* Braley, R., Fritz, R., Van Son, C. R. & Schmitter-Edgecombe, M. (2019). Prompting technology and persons with dementia: the significance of context and communication. //The Gerontologist//, 59(1), 101-111.
* Victor, C. R. (2012). Loneliness in care homes: a neglected area of research? //Aging Health//, 8(6), 637-646.
